Abstract
\par
The assessment of agricultural performance in the last decades has been forced to move away from a single criterion of productivity towards broader viewpoints. This article focuses on the feasibility of agri-environmental indicators that are aimed to be used for international comparisons. Indicators of two widely known international organizations, OECD and the Commission of European Communities, were evaluated from the viewpoint of national case study. There exist already plenty of methodology to describe agri-environmental development, which mean that future decision-makers will be better informed about the changes of agricultural systems. Data sets are, however, heterogeneous, which needs be carefully acknowledged by the users. Moreover, characteristic feature of agri-environmental indicators is, that they are producing information in a given problem structuring. Therefore, they are unable to recognize the specific properties of each case study. They are providing relevant statistics, but are still missing for the framework, which would open the meaning of numbers and show the relevance of notices within the particular case study.
\par
Introduction
\par
Agricultural policies have recently experienced major reformulations. Traditionally, they have been characterized by their productivist goals, which have also been carried out extremely successfully. During the past century, food prices have been falling and production per capita has been steadily increasing eventually resulting in also the overproduction of agricultural products (Parris, 2002). In European Union (EU) the current policy reforms are aiming towards increased multifunctionality of agricultural production. This means that agriculture is now expected to take care of the environment and landscape, support rural viability and manage cultural heritage being, however, also a competitive branch of industry (Potter and Erwin, 1999).
\par
The policy challenge for balancing between various objectives of agricultural policies is a major one. Relevant and up-to-date information systems are one of the cornerstones in order to succeed in this task. In that sense, currently prevailing information systems have been found as inadequate as they are mostly focusing on economic performance of the sector. Changing policy priorities requires an overhaul of the information systems with more attention being paid to ecological and social features of agricultural practices (Barnes, 2002, CEC, 2000, CEC, 2001).
\par
Agri-environmental indicators (AEIs) are one of these tools to address current development paths of agricultural production in broader terms. Specific to these instruments is their purpose towards ultimate simplification. They are aiming to provide ``an easily understood picture about the main policy trends by a selected number of key figures'' (CEC, 1999).
\par
These tendencies towards simplified measurements of agricultural policy have been supported by the present changes in administrative culture, where openness and efficiency of decision-making are now emphasized. Policy actions should be efficiently targeted and accountable so that the funds used by the public sector would provide the highest possible added value for tax payers (OECD, 2001). The requirement has resulted in rapid growth of contracting with in-built performance targets and linkage of policy incentives, rewards and sanctions (Sandersson, 2000). All of these are requiring quantitative information, which is in easily manageable format enabling the comparison between various activities and circumstances.
\par
Especially, there is currently a lack of tools, which would enable the comparison of agricultural performance among various countries and regions. These kind of measurements have gained importance because of the shift towards supranational policies and harmonization of national practices, which has especially occurred in European countries (Jokinen, 2004). There is also growing number of multilateral commitments (e.g. the Kyoto protocol to reduce greenhouse gas emissions and convention of Rio de Janeiro to protect biological diversity) at global level, where new informative tools are required in order to carry out the political obligations.
\par
Developing of international systems for measuring environmental performance of agriculture has been recently promoted by several organizations. Two organizations will be here especially focused. Both of them are organizations that are likely to have major impacts on the design of future policy actions.
\par
OECD has been one of the early pioneers with Agri-Environmental Indicators (AEIs). Their focus has been on the methodological design of assessment methods, which has been carried out in a mutual communication with scientists and policy makers. OECD was also the first organization to publish an international report to review the agri-environmental performance of various countries (Environmental Indicators for agriculture; OECD, 2001).
\par
In EU, the developing of AEIs got started along the strengthening of sustainability policies of the Union. It was decided, that environmental concerns needs to be integrated into all sectoral policies and the achievement of these efforts to be reported regularly. In agriculture, the first step towards integration of environmental concerns with agricultural policies was taken with the 1992 CAP (Common Agricultural Policy) reform, which introduced several new concepts and management activities of environmental protection. The integration was still deepened by the recent revitalization of CAP (Agenda 2000 for 2000--2006), where environmental concerns got now more influence and were financially prioritised (Zalidis et al., 2004). Accordingly, the methods to assess the efficiency of current policy actions were given increasing impetus (Winter and Gaskell, 1998, Jokinen, 2004).
\par
AEIs are one of the official formats of EU to report about the environmental achievements of agricultural sector among EU-countries. Five services of the Commission-Eurostat, the Agriculture DG, the Environment DG, Joint Research Centre and European Environmental Agency (EEA)-have been specially involved with these efforts besides large group of specialists, who are presenting member countries. The first proposal of EU-indicators was introduced in 2000 (CEC, 2000) and since that, it has been constantly improved in order to reach concise methodologies for international comparisons (CEC, 2001, EEA, 2003).
\par
The task of producing internationally comparable data sets about the agricultural performance is, evidently, politically very important task, but also, scientifically quite ambiguous. These data sets are always aggregated and are likely to include various sources for uncertainty. Especially, describing of the ecological changes is difficult as these phenomena are always affected by the dynamics of local ecosystems. The functional properties -- strengths and weaknesses -- of these data sets are still inadequately known and managed (Hezri, 2004). In this article, we aim to study this question by highlighting international indicators from the perspective of Finnish case study. Basically, the question is, whether or not, it is possible to apply a given standard set of indicator for performing a meaningful assessment of the performance of agricultural system at national level.
